.TH CHMOD 1 "September 2026" "mitos-utils 0.1.0" "MITOS User Commands"
.SH NAME
chmod \- change file mode bits
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B chmod
[\fB\-R\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR] \fIMODE\fR \fIFILE\fR...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Change the mode of each FILE to MODE, given as an octal literal (e.g. \fB755\fR) or a symbolic clause list (e.g. \fBu+x\fR, \fBgo\-w\fR, \fBa=r,u+w\fR).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-R, \-\-recursive
Change files and directories recursively.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Every mitos-utils command additionally accepts \fB\-\-help\fR (print a usage line and exit) and \fB\-\-version\fR. Commands that take file/path arguments also accept \fB\-\-\fR to mark the end of options, so a file literally named e.g. \fB\-oddfile\fR can still be referenced.
.SH NOTES
With \fB\-R\fR on Linux, traversal is TOCTOU-hardened (common::safewalk); a symbolic spec is computed relative to each entry's own current mode, not one flat value copied onto the whole tree. \fBX\fR (conditional execute) and \fBs\fR/\fBt\fR symbolic bits are not supported. See mitos-utils-security(7).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on a general failure (a listed file could not be processed, etc.), 2 on a usage error (missing or invalid arguments).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mitos\-box (1),
.BR mitos\-utils\-compat (7),
.BR mitos\-utils\-integration (7)
